{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 15, :comments ["<b>Why then should I find favor in my lord's eyes</b> — Why should you do me a kindness that I do not need? 'I find favor in your eyes' — yet you do not repay me now with any recompense; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Genesis 33:15). And Jacob's intention was that he had no desire for them or for their company at all, and all the more so since he intended to go by another road. And our Sages saw in this yet another lesson: they said (Bereshit Rabbah 78:15), Rabbi Yannai, when he would go up to the government, would study this pericope and would not take Romans along with him. Once he did not study it and took Romans along with him, and they did not reach Akko until he had sold off his travel pouch. This is because they had a tradition that this pericope deals with exile: when he would come to Rome in the court of the kings of Edom on matters of the community, he would study this pericope to follow the advice of the wise elder, for from him the generations would learn and do likewise. And he would not accept a retinue of Roman men to escort him, for they draw near only for their own benefit and leave a person's property ownerless."], :book :genesis, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {0 "The Aramaic paraphrase of the Bereshit Rabbah midrash is rendered into English directly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:39:36.317830444Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}